## Proof

**Fixed points of fⁿ.** For z ∈ S, fⁿ(z) = z^{mⁿ}. So z is fixed by fⁿ iff z^{mⁿ} = z, i.e., z^{mⁿ−1} = 1 (since z ≠ 0 on S). These are the (mⁿ−1)-th roots of unity, giving exactly

F(n) := |Fix(fⁿ) ∩ S| = mⁿ − 1

fixed points (valid since m > 1 ⟹ mⁿ − 1 ≥ 1).

**Möbius inversion.** Let P(n) denote the number of points of *least* period n. Then

F(n) = Σ_{d | n} P(d),

so by Möbius inversion:

P(n) = Σ_{d | n} μ(n/d) · F(d) = Σ_{d | n} μ(n/d)(m^d − 1).

Since 1989 > 1, the constant terms vanish: Σ_{d|n} μ(n/d) = 0, so

P(1989) = Σ_{d | 1989} μ(1989/d) · m^d.

**Factorization of 1989.** We have 1989 = 3² · 13 · 17.

**Identifying nonzero terms.** The Möbius function μ(1989/d) is nonzero iff 1989/d is squarefree. Writing d = 3^a · 13^b · 17^c (with a ∈ {0,1,2}, b,c ∈ {0,1}), the quotient 1989/d = 3^{2−a} · 13^{1−b} · 17^{1−c} is squarefree iff 2−a ∈ {0,1}, i.e., **a ∈ {1,2}** (while b, c remain free). This gives 2·2·2 = 8 divisors:

| d | 1989/d | μ(1989/d) | Sign |
|---|--------|-----------|------|
| 3 | 663 = 3·13·17 | (−1)³ = −1 | − |
| 39 = 3·13 | 51 = 3·17 | (−1)² = +1 | + |
| 51 = 3·17 | 39 = 3·13 | (−1)² = +1 | + |
| 663 = 3·13·17 | 3 | (−1)¹ = −1 | − |
| 9 = 3² | 221 = 13·17 | (−1)² = +1 | + |
| 117 = 9·13 | 17 | (−1)¹ = −1 | − |
| 153 = 9·17 | 13 | (−1)¹ = −1 | − |
| 1989 = 9·13·17 | 1 | (+1) | + |

**Computing the sum:**

P(1989) = (−1)m³ + (+1)m³⁹ + (+1)m⁵¹ + (−1)m⁶⁶³ + (+1)m⁹ + (−1)m¹¹⁷ + (−1)m¹⁵³ + (+1)m¹⁹⁸⁹

$$\boxed{N = m^{1989} - m^{663} - m^{153} - m^{117} + m^{51} + m^{39} + m^{9} - m^{3}}$$

**Verification of non-negativity.** Setting t = m³, the expression becomes

N = t⁶⁶³ − t²²¹ − t⁵¹ − t³⁹ + t¹⁷ + t¹³ + t³ − t = Σ_{d | 663} μ(d) · t^{663/d},

where 663 = 3·13·17 is squarefree. This equals 1989 · M₁₉₈₉(m), where M_n(x) = (1/n)Σ_{d|n} μ(d) x^{n/d} is the **necklace polynomial**, which counts aperiodic necklaces of length n over an alphabet of size x. For integer m ≥ 2, M₁₉₈₉(m) is a non-negative integer, so N ≥ 0. ✓

**Divisibility check.** Since periodic points of least period 1989 form orbits of size 1989 under f, N must be divisible by 1989. Indeed N = 1989 · M₁₉₈₉(m), confirming divisibility. ✓

**Sanity check with small case.** For m = 2, n = 2: P(2) = μ(2)(2¹−1) + μ(1)(2²−1) = (−1)(1) + (1)(3) = 2. Directly: Fix(f²) gives z³ = 1 (3 roots), Fix(f) gives z = 1 (1 root), so least period 2 gives 3 − 1 = 2. ✓

